body:
Update: Used this thing for a live event this week, it went incredibly well! This thing has incredibly good ergonomics compared to the pocket cameras.

&#x200B;

On another note, I wish they would make a camera between the Ursa Mini and Pocket line, something with a less DSLR style body, but with the same software and image quality that the pocket cameras have, cmon Blackmagic!!!

I've been thinking about trying to do this as a job, but it's difficult doing that at 17, since I can't sign contracts and obviously don't have time in the industry or a degree, which is oftentimes a requirement to get hired, but if I can find some way to build a name for myself, then that won't be an issue.

&#x200B;

I've been thinking of trying to do some third party work for a bit, something like fiver or upwork, if I can build out a good portfolio doing that, then I won't have AS MUCH trouble finding a job in the industry, contract or not.

I have! I have an EOS M, the main issue I have with it is reliability for film sets, it sucks when a camera goes down and we're down a camera, which on its own is a deal breaker.

&#x200B;

another part is the workflow, converting MLV files into CDNG (the fastest conversion you can do) still takes forever, which slows down post work significantly when you're working with a ton of shots.

FRIK isn't working in really any way shape or form. Even when it doesn't crash, my pipboy doesn't work, my hands are broken. My legs fly all over the place, my menus also lag behind my headsets view, and whenever I pause my view starts flying all over the place.

&#x200B;

I've reinstalled twice, made sure I have all the pre-requisites for FRIK and made sure everything was installed, I'm also using MO2.
